speechtext:
There have been forwarded to me three declarations, one by the mayor of the township, Pierre Tardif, who was chairman at that meeting. This declaration is as follows:
I, the undersigned, Pierre Tardif, of King-sey Falls, county of Drummond, and mayor of the place, solemnly declare: that I have taken communication of the Montreal ' Gazette,' dated the 22nd day of November, 1910, reporting that in a course of a parliamentary debate which took place at Ottawa on the previous day, Mr. Monk said that a prominent notary of the town of Richmond, named Begin, in a speech made during the last campaign in Drummond and Arthabaska declared that * if necessary the Canadian navy could be used to pound England ';
That the notary Begin who made a speech here on Sunday, previous to the polling day, is not of Richmond but of Windsor Mills;
That I was chairman of the meeting;
That the speakers at that meeting were Messrs. Drepeau and Lamarche on the Nationalist side and on the Liberal side Messrs. Guillemette and Morin said a few words, and notary Begin made the main speech on the Liberal side and Mr. Lamarche had the reply;
That I am positive that notary Begin has not used such words as reported in the Montreal ' Gazette,' nor any others to the same effect, nor has he expressed anything conveying such an idea.
And I make this solemn declaration believing the same to be true and knowing that it is of the same force and effect as if made under oath under the Canada Evidence Act, and I have signed. One word erased is null.
Declared before me at Kingsey Falls, this 25th day of November, 1910.
